## Worktree Rule

All PR and feature branch work MUST be done through an isolated worktree.

### Always Do

- **MUST call `EnterWorktree` before starting any branch or PR work.** Never edit code on a named branch in the main checkout — always get a fresh worktree first.
- After the PR is merged (or abandoned), call `ExitWorktree` to clean up.
- Session file commits (`/pythia-session save`) are the one kind of commit that may land on a feature branch: a **worktree session** commits its session file directly to the feature branch, where it rides the PR and lands on `main` at merge. Only a **main-checkout session** (rare) uses the branch-hop dance to commit directly on `main`.

### Never Do

- NEVER make code edits on a feature branch in the main checkout.
- NEVER commit code changes (non-session) directly to `main`.

### Why

Worktrees give each PR an isolated filesystem, preventing stale state, accidental cross-contamination between branches, and the "wrong branch" class of errors. The `bgIsolation: worktree` setting in `.claude/settings.json` enforces this for background agents automatically; the rule above extends it to foreground sessions.

## Backend Deploys Are Manual

api.sidira.ai does NOT auto-deploy; only the Amplify frontend rebuilds on push
to `main`. **After any merge to `main` that touches `backend/`, prompt the user
to redeploy the backend** — otherwise the live app 404s on the new routes while
prod silently serves the previous build. Include this in the merge wrap-up:

```
~/.npm-global/bin/codex exec "Read deploy/REDEPLOY.md in this repo and execute the backend redeploy for api.sidira.ai. If a step needs the AWS console, walk me through it."
```

`deploy/REDEPLOY.md` is the agent-executable runbook (staleness check,
`SIDIRA_AUTO_MIGRATE` decision for Alembic migrations, SSM deploy command,
verification). If the merged PR added a migration, say so explicitly in the
reminder — that changes the runbook's step 2.

## Design System

`DESIGN.md` at the repo root is the canonical source of truth for every
visual, UI, and brand decision. Read it before:

- Adding or editing any UI component.
- Touching colors, typography, spacing, radius, or motion in CSS / Tailwind.
- Creating a new SVG, image, or marketing surface.
- Building a new page or screen.
- Reviewing a PR that touches `frontend/` for visual correctness.

The mark, the wordmark, and the lockup files live in
`frontend/public/brand/` and `frontend/app/icon.svg`. Reuse them; do not
recreate them. The dual-color mark is the **standard mark on every surface —
in-product nav and dashboard included** (DESIGN.md §1.3, ticket #35). There is
no monochrome-vs-dual or brand-surfaces-only split: the mark rests dual-color
everywhere (favicon · app icon · public site · about / press / anniversary ·
product nav · dashboard · console shell). The legacy monochrome mark/lockup
remain in the repo only as fallbacks. The in-product aperture-close animation
(DESIGN.md §9.2) still applies during simulation runs and the decision-commit
beat.

When QAing or reviewing UI changes, flag any code that violates DESIGN.md
(refuse list in §10) or that deviates from the locked specs without an
accompanying DESIGN.md update in the same PR. The design system and the code
that consumes it stay in lockstep.

