   159	
   160	## 4. Color
   161	
   162	### 4.1 Architecture — three layers, three meanings
   163	
   164	| Layer | Token | Hex | Carries |
   165	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
   166	| **Structure** = Sid | `ink` | `#0E1A2B` | the entire ink layer — mark, wordmark, body text, rules, structural foundation. Already navy-leaning. Sid is the thing everything else stands on. |
   167	| **Signal** = brand-functional | `signal` | `#C8FF3D` | live state · the instrument is reading · oscilloscope-phosphor accent. No personal meaning. Countable budget: lime lives on **at most three carriers per screen** — a glass well's live traces (the well counts as one carrier; §9.1 sanctions two traces inside it), the §9.5 live carrier, and the active state of one control. Never on two adjacent elements, never as fill, never as background. |
   168	| **Vital** = Avira | `avira` | `#D696C8` | secondary accent for narrowly-scoped narrative surfaces — see §4.4. |
   169	
   170	**Lime's primary home is glass.** On glass (`#0A1421`) signal lime reaches
   171	~15.7:1 contrast and reads as phosphor — that is where it lives. On paper, lime
   172	requires an ink companion stroke at ≥3:1 contrast (this codifies the existing
   173	underdraw practice as law; bare lime on cream fails at ~1:1).
   174	
   175	**Phosphor persistence:** lime traces on glass get a 5 px / 18%-opacity
   176	underdraw of the same path. Never CSS glow, blur, or `drop-shadow`.
   177	
   178	### 4.2 Surface and text tokens
   179	
   180	| Token | Hex / value | Use |
   181	| --- | --- | --- |
   182	| `paper` | `#F4EFE6` | primary surface — archival-tungsten warm off-white |
   183	| `paper-elevated` | `#FAF6EE` | cards · modals · elevated panels |
   184	| `paper-recessed` | `#EBE5D8` | inputs · code blocks · receded panels |
   185	| `rule` | `rgba(14,26,43,0.16)` | hairline dividers, **`0.75px` CSS** — a true hairline (replaces solid `#D6CFC0`; unit law in §7.2 — never `pt`) |
   186	| `ink` | `#0E1A2B` | primary text · mark · lines |
   187	| `ink-muted` | `#4A5668` | secondary text · axis labels · captions |
   188	
   189	#### Glass — the instrument display
   190	
   191	| Token | Value | Use |
   192	| --- | --- | --- |
   193	| `glass` | `#0A1421` | the dark display surface — **live computed readings only** |
   194	| `glass-rule` | `rgba(244,239,230,0.08)` | hairlines on glass |
   195	| `glass-text` | `#F4EFE6` | primary text on glass |
   196	| `glass-muted` | `rgba(244,239,230,0.55)` | secondary text on glass |
   197	
   198	Glass is a **component surface, not a theme**. It is sanctioned ONLY for live
   199	instrument readings: confidence traces, CI axes, running states, live
   200	telemetry readouts. Never for chrome, navigation, tables, text content, cards,
   201	or marketing. If glass spreads, the brand bet dissolves into a generic dark
   202	dashboard — this rule is load-bearing. Glass wells take radius 4 and a 1 px
   203	`glass-rule` border.
   204	
   205	#### Layering
   206	
   207	- Recessed: `inset 0 1px 2px rgba(14,26,43,0.07)`
   208	- Elevated: hairline border + `inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,0.55)` top highlight
   209	- Drop shadows remain banned.
   210	- Maximum **3 paper levels + glass** on any screen.
   211	
   212	### 4.3 Semantic tokens
   213	
   214	| Token | Hex | Use |
   215	| --- | --- | --- |
   216	| `alert` | `#E84A2C` | error · weakness-flagged |
   217	| `warn` | `#D9A300` | underpowered · caution |
   218	| `success` | `#2F6B3F` | confirmed · ship-candidate affordances · positive deltas |
   219	| `signal` | `#C8FF3D` | live · settled · evidence (see §4.1) |
   220	
   221	`success` exists so that no surface ever invents its own green — it closes the
   222	root-cause gap behind the off-token green/blue/red hexes that accumulated in
   223	production. Token sweep happens in the rollout's PR 2.
   224	
   225	**Semantic colors are chip and accent colors, not prose colors.** Prose and
   226	body text are always `ink` or `ink-muted`. What each token may touch:
   227	
   228	| Token | Text? | Accent (borders · bands · markers)? |
   229	| --- | --- | --- |
   230	| `warn` | **never** — ≈2.0:1 on paper, fails WCAG at any size (Codex catch, PR #174 — now law). Mid-range score text falls back to `ink-muted` | yes — this is its only job |
   231	| `alert` | short mono designators and chips only, always co-signaled by a border or the wording itself (≈3.4:1 — below AA for small text; see §7.8). Never body text | yes |
   232	| `success` | short mono designators and chips (≈5.6:1 — AA) | yes |
   233	| `signal` | never text on paper (§4.1 — needs the ink companion); on glass per §4.1 | per §4.1 only |
   234	
   235	### 4.4 Sid + Avira — sanctioned use
   236	
   237	The personal-meaning colors appear in two color states of the mark (§1.3) and
   238	in five documented surfaces. Anywhere else is wrong.
   239	
   240	| Surface | Sid (`#2C5577`) | Avira (`#D696C8`) |
   241	| --- | --- | --- |
   242	| Founder-mode mark (3-and-3 split) | 3 left-side blades | 3 right-side blades |
   243	| Cofounder / about page | (ink is sid-coded; no extra token used) | one rose hairline rule above founder bios |
   244	| First-ever simulation run | — | one-time rose pulse on the "begin" button |
   245	| Persona chart series | — | the "warm" archetype gets rose as its series color |
   246	| Anniversary / launch hero | — | a single rose word in a marketing headline |
   247	
   248	Forbidden uses for `avira` (as a **standalone** accent — the resting duo
   249	mark is exempt, see below):
   250	- as a button fill
   251	- as a primary CTA color
   252	- as a standalone accent (a lone rose tick / fill / chrome element) on the
   253	  dashboard / result page / wizard / agent chat — the duo *mark* resting
   254	  there is sanctioned (§1.3), but avira may not appear as a separate accent
   255	- as a standalone accent in the favicon / app icon / nav lockup — its only
   256	  sanctioned appearance there is as half of the duo mark (§1.3 everywhere-
   257	  standard mark, §9.2 animation keyframes)
   258	- anywhere a buyer's first impression is being formed *as a standalone accent*
   259	
   260	The duo mark resting everywhere — favicon, nav, and dashboard included — is
   261	the §1.3 standard, not a contradiction of these rules: the rules above govern
   262	`avira` as a *standalone* accent, never the two-color mark itself. This line
